From 574c8fa312e923fc7f791bbf3f242d94c4aeb271 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jerrod <jerrod.uno@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 19:06:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] d3d12: real occlusion query results (BEGIN/END pairing,
 deferred write)

Host occlusion queries were in place but the guest almost never got
their result; it got the fake count (1000 = fully visible) instead:

1. D3D gives BEGIN and END their own sample count structs. In Crackdown
   END's is 0x20 bytes before BEGIN's (BEGIN zpd at 0x1D9531A0, END at
   0x1D953180); the GPU writes a counter snapshot into each and D3D takes
   END minus BEGIN. The handler required the same address for both, so
   every END was a "mismatch" and got the fake count. Now END pairs with
   the active query, BEGIN's struct is zeroed (snapshot 0), and END's
   gets the host count.
2. The result was read right after submitting the query, before the GPU
   had run it, so the fence check failed and the fallback was written.
   Now ended queries wait in a FIFO and their results are written when
   their submission completes (checked on every ZPD and on every pass of
   the idle loop through the new CommandProcessor::OnWaitPoll). Until
   then the struct keeps D3D's 0xFFFFFEED marker, so the guest sees the
   query as not finished, as on hardware. PrepareForWait submits the
   open work when results are pending, so a guest spinning in GetData
   can't deadlock.
3. EndSubmission ended any active query and dropped it, and the next END
   then called DisableHostOcclusionQueries, which turned host queries
   off for the rest of the session. Now a guest query that spans
   submissions is split into one host query per submission and the
   counts are summed; a mismatch abandons only that query.

Seen in Crackdown: traffic light coronas showed through buildings.
Verified on PC (RTX 5080, 2026-10-11): with
--query_occlusion_fake_sample_count=0 they vanish everywhere (and the
ocean flickers); with this fix they are hidden behind buildings and
visible in the open, and the ocean is fine. An F10-triggered log of 400
query events confirmed the BEGIN/END address pattern.

The Vulkan backend has the same design and is not changed here.
